Cloud, Security & IoT Deployment Blueprint
This blueprint deploys the fixed scheduler and Banker's-Algorithm engine from Part 1 unchanged, including its jobs.py workload, FCFS/SJF/SRTF/RR/Priority implementations, Peterson synchronization demonstration, banker, and address translators.

Architecture and flows
Choose Client-Server: the three zone controllers are clients and the central Smart City Operations dashboard/API is the server. It provides location transparency because operators use one dashboard rather than zone-specific endpoints; it scales by adding server/API workers; centralized identity and audit policy improve operational control; and the server is a potential single point of failure, mitigated through multi-AZ replicas and load balancing. It is less fault-tolerant than a peer mesh during a total central outage, but zones buffer events locally and retry.

A real-time public-safety alert is asynchronous MQTT publish/subscribe: MQTT is lightweight, supports rapid fan-out, and avoids blocking a controller while the dashboard is unavailable.

A daily full sensor-log upload is asynchronous HTTPS object-upload work: it is large, retryable archival traffic, and HTTPS supplies authenticated encrypted transport without needing an immediate dashboard response.

Network boundary
Use one VPC with three private zone subnets (Zone-A, Zone-B, Zone-C), plus a separate dashboard/ingestion subnet. The VPC gives logical isolation and subnet-level customizability for CIDRs, route tables, and security rules without operating three separate VPCs. Each zone subnet's security group permits egress only to the dashboard-ingestion security group on MQTT/TLS and HTTPS; it contains no inbound rule from either other zone security group. The route tables also omit direct zone-to-zone routes. Those security-group and routing rules—not the dashboard—enforce the Zone-A/Zone-B boundary.

